WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.310
Welcome to the first of
three lectures for the

2
00:00:03.350 --> 00:00:06.569
aircraft systems exam of
Vehicles Aeroespacials.

3
00:00:06.619 --> 00:00:10.811
The exam covers two systems
of the Airbus A320: the whole

4
00:00:10.851 --> 00:00:14.663
pneumatic system, ATA chapter
36, and the hydraulic

5
00:00:14.703 --> 00:00:19.601
system, ATA chapter 29, up to
and including the Green system.

6
00:00:19.651 --> 00:00:23.508
This part explains what the pneumatic
system is for, where the air

7
00:00:23.548 --> 00:00:27.465
comes from, who uses it, and how
the crew and the computers control

8
00:00:27.505 --> 00:00:32.284
it; part two goes inside every valve,
and part three covers the hydraulics.

9
00:00:33.934 --> 00:00:35.735
First, what to expect.

10
00:00:35.785 --> 00:00:38.972
The scope agreed for this exam
is the whole pneumatic deck

11
00:00:39.012 --> 00:00:42.142
and the hydraulic deck up to
the Green system, which ends

12
00:00:42.182 --> 00:00:45.981
on page 12, so the Yellow and
Blue system details are out.

13
00:00:46.031 --> 00:00:50.155
The 2020 systems quiz, the
closest past paper, is a set of

14
00:00:50.195 --> 00:00:54.256
statements to mark as true or false:
for example, whether the high

15
00:00:54.296 --> 00:01:00.115
pressure valve opens at 36 psi, or which
reservoir is filled from which panel.

16
00:01:00.165 --> 00:01:03.949
Older papers also ask you to
explain how a component works,

17
00:01:03.989 --> 00:01:07.513
such as the temperature
limiting thermostat, or to list

18
00:01:07.553 --> 00:01:12.764
conditions, such as when the pressure
regulating valve closes electrically.

19
00:01:12.814 --> 00:01:17.333
Some old papers ask about the
MD87, which belonged to an earlier

20
00:01:17.373 --> 00:01:22.881
version of the course; the current decks
only cover the A320, so skip those.

21
00:01:22.931 --> 00:01:27.710
The winning skill is precise: for every
valve, know its default position without

22
00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:32.918
air, the pressures and temperatures that
move it, and which element commands it.

23
00:01:34.568 --> 00:01:37.706
The pneumatic system of an
aircraft takes compressed air

24
00:01:37.746 --> 00:01:40.942
from one of its sources, the
engines, the auxiliary power

25
00:01:40.982 --> 00:01:45.134
unit or a ground cart, and delivers
it to the systems that need it.

26
00:01:45.184 --> 00:01:50.614
On the A320 the air is delivered
regulated to 44 psi and about 200

27
00:01:50.654 --> 00:01:55.658
degrees Celsius: those two numbers are
the reference values of the whole system.

28
00:01:55.708 --> 00:01:59.008
The main user is the air
conditioning and pressurisation

29
00:01:59.048 --> 00:02:02.347
system, chapter 21, then
engine starting and the

30
00:02:02.387 --> 00:02:05.507
anti-ice of the wings and
the engine intakes, chapter

31
00:02:05.547 --> 00:02:10.072
30; the deck's generic list also
mentions the vertical stabiliser.

32
00:02:10.122 --> 00:02:13.263
Bleed air also pressurises
the hydraulic reservoirs,

33
00:02:13.303 --> 00:02:16.262
so that the pumps never
cavitate, and the potable

34
00:02:16.302 --> 00:02:20.201
water tank, so that water
reaches the galleys and toilets.

35
00:02:20.251 --> 00:02:24.271
Finally, it can provide motive
power, for example to actuate thrust

36
00:02:24.311 --> 00:02:28.998
reversers or to drive air-driven
hydraulic pumps, and on the A320 it

37
00:02:29.038 --> 00:02:33.569
also heats the aft cargo hold, which
is needed to carry live animals.

38
00:02:35.219 --> 00:02:37.833
There are three sources of compressed air.

39
00:02:37.883 --> 00:02:41.163
The engines are the main source
in flight: from their high

40
00:02:41.203 --> 00:02:45.914
compressor stages the air comes
at between 50 and 410 psi and

41
00:02:45.954 --> 00:02:51.581
between 180 and 540 degrees
Celsius, from idle to cruise, far

42
00:02:51.621 --> 00:02:56.321
too hot and too variable to use directly,
which is why it must be regulated.

43
00:02:56.371 --> 00:03:00.464
The auxiliary power unit, the APU,
is a small gas turbine in the tail

44
00:03:00.504 --> 00:03:04.538
that supplies electrical and pneumatic
power on the ground, for engine

45
00:03:04.578 --> 00:03:09.876
starting and air conditioning, and in
flight as a backup, up to 20 000 feet.

46
00:03:09.926 --> 00:03:13.098
On the ground, a pneumatic
cart can be connected through

47
00:03:13.138 --> 00:03:16.252
a quick-release access panel
under the fuselage, onto a

48
00:03:16.292 --> 00:03:19.912
connector with a check valve
that stops the air escaping.

49
00:03:19.962 --> 00:03:24.187
The notes stress that the APU is
on the Minimum Equipment List, the

50
00:03:24.227 --> 00:03:28.329
document that says which items may
be inoperative and for how long:

51
00:03:28.369 --> 00:03:34.221
deferred defects of category B, C
and D allow 3, 10 and 120 days.

52
00:03:34.271 --> 00:03:37.478
Two drawing conventions: odd
numbers are on the left and

53
00:03:37.518 --> 00:03:40.957
even numbers on the right, and
both the APU and the ground

54
00:03:40.997 --> 00:03:45.058
connection feed the left side,
side 1, of the crossbleed duct.

55
00:03:46.708 --> 00:03:50.982
This is the generic layout from the
deck, and every airliner follows it.

56
00:03:51.032 --> 00:03:53.963
Each engine feeds its own
side of a common manifold

57
00:03:54.003 --> 00:03:57.791
through its bleed valves;
pneumatic ducts are drawn dashed.

58
00:03:57.841 --> 00:04:03.392
A crossbleed, or isolation, valve in the
middle can join or separate the two sides.

59
00:04:03.442 --> 00:04:06.760
The APU joins the manifold
on one side, through its own

60
00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:10.979
bleed valve and a check valve that
prevents reverse flow into it.

61
00:04:11.029 --> 00:04:15.252
And the users are connected along the
manifold: the air conditioning packs,

62
00:04:15.292 --> 00:04:20.133
the wing anti-ice, the engine starters
and the pressurisation of other systems.

63
00:04:20.183 --> 00:04:23.980
The point of the architecture is
flexibility with isolation: any

64
00:04:24.020 --> 00:04:28.567
source can feed any user, and a failed
or leaking side can be cut off.

65
00:04:30.217 --> 00:04:34.375
Now the heart of the system: how
the air is taken from the engine.

66
00:04:34.425 --> 00:04:38.433
Normally the air comes from an
intermediate compressor stage, the fifth

67
00:04:38.473 --> 00:04:42.766
stage of the high pressure compressor
on the A320, through a check

68
00:04:42.806 --> 00:04:47.604
valve, the intermediate pressure check
valve, that lets it flow only outwards.

69
00:04:47.654 --> 00:04:52.338
At idle or low thrust, stage 5 cannot
give enough pressure, so the air is

70
00:04:52.378 --> 00:04:57.826
taken from a higher stage, stage 9,
through the high pressure valve, the HPV.

71
00:04:57.876 --> 00:05:01.152
Both paths join upstream
of the pressure regulating

72
00:05:01.192 --> 00:05:05.703
valve, the PRV, which delivers
44 psi to the system.

73
00:05:05.753 --> 00:05:09.452
The air is then cooled in the
precooler, a heat exchanger where

74
00:05:09.492 --> 00:05:13.132
cold air bled from the fan flows
around the tubes carrying the

75
00:05:13.172 --> 00:05:16.990
hot bleed air; the two streams
never mix, and the used fan air is

76
00:05:17.030 --> 00:05:21.416
dumped overboard; a fan air valve
controls how much fan air is used.

77
00:05:21.466 --> 00:05:24.270
Pressure and temperature
sensors at several points let

78
00:05:24.310 --> 00:05:27.610
the bleed computers monitor
and control the whole chain.

79
00:05:29.260 --> 00:05:31.509
The deck shows the
typical bleed regulating

80
00:05:31.549 --> 00:05:35.148
valve, and the A320 valves
follow the same pattern.

81
00:05:35.198 --> 00:05:38.007
A butterfly in the duct
is moved by a pneumatic

82
00:05:38.047 --> 00:05:41.159
actuator, while the valve
is controlled electrically

83
00:05:41.199 --> 00:05:45.671
through a solenoid: electrically
controlled, pneumatically actuated.

84
00:05:45.721 --> 00:05:48.796
The actuator compares a reference
pressure with the pressure

85
00:05:48.836 --> 00:05:52.014
downstream of the butterfly: if
the downstream pressure is too

86
00:05:52.054 --> 00:05:55.232
high it closes the butterfly a
little, and if it is too low it

87
00:05:55.272 --> 00:05:59.307
opens it, so the butterfly hunts
constantly around the reference value.

88
00:05:59.357 --> 00:06:02.607
The valve therefore has three
functions: to switch the

89
00:06:02.647 --> 00:06:05.654
bleed on and off, to regulate
the pressure, and to

90
00:06:05.694 --> 00:06:09.761
protect the system against high
temperature and reverse flow.

91
00:06:09.811 --> 00:06:13.878
Position switches send discrete signals
which, together with the pressures,

92
00:06:13.918 --> 00:06:18.485
are checked by a built-in test to confirm
the real position of the butterfly.

93
00:06:20.135 --> 00:06:22.424
Let us look at each user in turn.

94
00:06:22.474 --> 00:06:26.042
Wing anti-ice sends hot bleed
air through a valve on each wing

95
00:06:26.082 --> 00:06:29.708
into a perforated duct along the
leading edge, which bathes its

96
00:06:29.748 --> 00:06:33.374
inside surface; telescopic ducts
with flexible joints carry the

97
00:06:33.414 --> 00:06:37.156
air to the slats, from slat 3
outwards, and over-pressure and

98
00:06:37.196 --> 00:06:42.085
over-temperature sensors let the valve
cut the supply if something goes wrong.

99
00:06:42.135 --> 00:06:45.329
Engine anti-ice is separate:
one or more valves on the fan

100
00:06:45.369 --> 00:06:48.340
case send hot air from the
engine itself to the intake

101
00:06:48.380 --> 00:06:52.354
cowl, and a pressure switch tells
the crew whether it is working.

102
00:06:52.404 --> 00:06:56.300
For engine start, air from the
APU, the ground cart or the other

103
00:06:56.340 --> 00:07:00.116
engine reaches the starter valve,
which opens and drives the air

104
00:07:00.156 --> 00:07:04.231
starter on the accessory gearbox:
the starter turns the high pressure

105
00:07:04.271 --> 00:07:08.822
spool, N2, which drags the fan, N1,
and fuel is injected once N2 has

106
00:07:08.862 --> 00:07:13.745
reached the right speed; turning the
engine without fuel is called cranking.

107
00:07:13.795 --> 00:07:17.623
The hydraulic reservoirs are
pressurised to 50 psi, normally

108
00:07:17.663 --> 00:07:21.611
from stage 8 of the high pressure
compressor of engine 1; the

109
00:07:21.651 --> 00:07:26.405
professor insists that the deck
drawing, which shows stage 9, is wrong.

110
00:07:26.455 --> 00:07:29.914
Finally the potable water tank,
the aft cargo heating and,

111
00:07:29.954 --> 00:07:34.769
above all, the air conditioning
packs, which belong to chapter 21.

112
00:07:36.419 --> 00:07:39.706
Here is the A320 layout with its users.

113
00:07:39.756 --> 00:07:44.215
Air is obtained from the two
engines, the APU, or a ground cart,

114
00:07:44.255 --> 00:07:50.126
selected with the ENG 1 BLEED, ENG
2 BLEED and APU BLEED pushbuttons.

115
00:07:50.176 --> 00:07:53.539
A crossbleed valve in the
transverse duct separates the two

116
00:07:53.579 --> 00:07:57.057
sides or joins them so that a
single source feeds every user;

117
00:07:57.097 --> 00:08:01.312
when it is closed, each engine feeds
its own air conditioning pack.

118
00:08:01.362 --> 00:08:06.274
On the deck's drawing, engine 1 also feeds
the heating of the aft cargo compartment.

119
00:08:06.324 --> 00:08:10.603
The system is designed so that the
APU bleed has priority: as soon

120
00:08:10.643 --> 00:08:14.668
as APU BLEED is selected, the
APU bleed valve opens and both

121
00:08:14.708 --> 00:08:19.759
engine bleed valves close automatically,
so the APU supplies everything.

122
00:08:19.809 --> 00:08:23.165
And remember that the APU and
the ground connection both

123
00:08:23.205 --> 00:08:26.892
enter on the left side, side
1, of the crossbleed duct.

124
00:08:28.542 --> 00:08:32.276
The crew controls the system
from the overhead panel 21VU,

125
00:08:32.316 --> 00:08:35.940
which the air conditioning and
the pneumatic systems share.

126
00:08:35.990 --> 00:08:40.600
Each engine bleed pushbutton has two
legends: with the bleed requested

127
00:08:40.640 --> 00:08:44.984
it is dark, a white OFF appears
when the crew deselects it, and an

128
00:08:45.024 --> 00:08:50.423
amber FAULT, together with an ECAM
message, signals a failure on that side.

129
00:08:50.473 --> 00:08:53.624
The APU BLEED pushbutton
is normally dark, shows a

130
00:08:53.664 --> 00:08:57.266
blue ON when selected, and
an amber FAULT on failure.

131
00:08:57.316 --> 00:09:01.270
The crossbleed valve is commanded
by a rotary selector with three

132
00:09:01.310 --> 00:09:05.080
positions, AUTO, OPEN and SHUT,
and the valve has two electric

133
00:09:05.120 --> 00:09:10.263
motors, one used by the automatic logic
and the other by the manual positions.

134
00:09:10.313 --> 00:09:14.926
In AUTO the crossbleed valve always
follows the APU bleed valve: it opens

135
00:09:14.966 --> 00:09:20.218
only when air is taken from the APU,
while OPEN and SHUT force it regardless.

136
00:09:20.268 --> 00:09:23.479
This is the Airbus dark cockpit:
in normal operation the

137
00:09:23.519 --> 00:09:26.730
panel is dark, and any light
means an abnormal condition

138
00:09:26.770 --> 00:09:30.719
or a non-standard selection that
must eventually be cancelled.

139
00:09:32.369 --> 00:09:35.112
Information reaches the
crew through the ECAM, the

140
00:09:35.152 --> 00:09:38.175
two central screens of the
main instrument panel.

141
00:09:38.225 --> 00:09:41.711
The upper one, the Engine and
Warning display, permanently

142
00:09:41.751 --> 00:09:45.297
shows the main engine
parameters, N1, N2, exhaust gas

143
00:09:45.337 --> 00:09:48.762
temperature and fuel flow,
plus the fuel on board and the

144
00:09:48.802 --> 00:09:53.529
high-lift surfaces, with the warning
and caution messages at the bottom.

145
00:09:53.579 --> 00:09:56.527
Class 1 alerts are red warnings
that require immediate

146
00:09:56.567 --> 00:10:00.013
crew action, and class 2 are
amber cautions that do not.

147
00:10:00.063 --> 00:10:03.894
Class 3 failures are not shown
to the crew at all: maintenance

148
00:10:03.934 --> 00:10:08.331
reads them on the multipurpose
control and display unit, the MCDU.

149
00:10:08.381 --> 00:10:12.170
The lower screen, the System
Display, shows synoptic pages,

150
00:10:12.210 --> 00:10:16.259
chosen automatically by flight
phase or with the pushbuttons of

151
00:10:16.299 --> 00:10:20.933
the ECAM control panel; the BLEED
pushbutton brings up this page.

152
00:10:20.983 --> 00:10:24.416
It shows the position of every
bleed valve, the pressures and

153
00:10:24.456 --> 00:10:28.060
temperatures, and a GND indication
that appears only when the

154
00:10:28.100 --> 00:10:32.664
aircraft is on the ground, detected by
the landing gear proximity sensors.

155
00:10:34.314 --> 00:10:36.955
Now an exercise with the
BLEED page: for each

156
00:10:36.995 --> 00:10:40.562
situation, picture the page
before the answer appears.

157
00:10:40.612 --> 00:10:45.269
One: normal cruise, both engine
bleeds on and the APU off.

158
00:10:45.319 --> 00:10:49.612
Both PRVs are open, drawn in
line with the duct; the HPVs are

159
00:10:49.652 --> 00:10:54.411
closed, because stage 5 alone is
enough; the crossbleed and the APU

160
00:10:54.451 --> 00:11:00.867
valve are closed; and each side reads
about 44 psi and about 200 degrees.

161
00:11:00.917 --> 00:11:03.587
Two: on the ground before
engine start, with

162
00:11:03.627 --> 00:11:07.118
APU BLEED on and the
crossbleed selector in AUTO.

163
00:11:07.168 --> 00:11:10.676
The APU bleed valve is open,
the crossbleed valve has

164
00:11:10.716 --> 00:11:14.224
followed it and is open,
both PRVs are closed because

165
00:11:14.264 --> 00:11:18.420
the APU has priority, and
the GND indication is shown.

166
00:11:18.470 --> 00:11:21.931
Three: an engine 2 bleed fault in cruise.

167
00:11:21.981 --> 00:11:26.359
PRV 2 is closed, ENG 2 BLEED
shows an amber FAULT with an ECAM

168
00:11:26.399 --> 00:11:30.737
caution, and the crossbleed stays
closed until the crew opens it.

169
00:11:30.787 --> 00:11:35.955
Four: one side shows a bleed
temperature of 250 degrees.

170
00:11:36.005 --> 00:11:41.027
That is well above the 200 plus or
minus 15 degree loop, so the TCT

171
00:11:41.067 --> 00:11:44.916
and fan air valve are not coping
and the TLT probe is already

172
00:11:44.956 --> 00:11:51.541
throttling the PRV; if it reaches 257
degrees, the BMC closes that PRV.

173
00:11:53.191 --> 00:11:57.044
The system is supervised by two
Bleed Monitoring Computers,

174
00:11:57.084 --> 00:12:01.201
BMC 1 and BMC 2, identical
and interchangeable, one for

175
00:12:01.241 --> 00:12:05.615
each side, permanently connected
to each other by data buses.

176
00:12:05.665 --> 00:12:09.092
Each receives electrical
signals from the pushbuttons,

177
00:12:09.132 --> 00:12:12.109
the crossbleed valve,
the engine and APU fire

178
00:12:12.149 --> 00:12:15.640
pushbuttons, the positions
of the bleed valves, the two

179
00:12:15.680 --> 00:12:19.107
pressure transmitters,
transferred pressure before the

180
00:12:19.147 --> 00:12:22.573
PRV and regulated pressure
after it, the temperature

181
00:12:22.613 --> 00:12:27.842
sensor and the leak detection loops of
its side, and some of the other side.

182
00:12:27.892 --> 00:12:32.013
With them it supervises the bleed, shuts
off its side if it detects a failure,

183
00:12:32.053 --> 00:12:36.671
and informs the crew through the ECAM and
maintenance through the fault system.

184
00:12:36.721 --> 00:12:41.549
Around them, the System Data Acquisition
Concentrators, the SDACs, collect

185
00:12:41.589 --> 00:12:46.096
the data for the Flight Warning
Computers, which generate the aural and

186
00:12:46.136 --> 00:12:50.899
visual alerts, and for the three display
computers, the DMCs, two working

187
00:12:50.939 --> 00:12:55.831
and one in standby; the CFDIU centralises
the faults of every system; the

188
00:12:55.871 --> 00:13:00.314
Engine Interface Units pass on the
data that each engine's own control

189
00:13:00.354 --> 00:13:06.084
unit collects; and the APU is controlled
by its Electronic Control Box, the ECB.

190
00:13:06.134 --> 00:13:10.526
Finally, leak detection loops run
alongside the ducts, the pylons and

191
00:13:10.566 --> 00:13:15.151
the APU duct; when one detects hot
air, the BMC isolates the leak by

192
00:13:15.191 --> 00:13:20.358
closing the corresponding bleed valve
before nearby components are damaged.

193
00:13:22.008 --> 00:13:25.876
The APU bleed has its own
logic, shown in this diagram.

194
00:13:25.926 --> 00:13:28.938
The APU supplies air directly
to the left side of the

195
00:13:28.978 --> 00:13:33.323
crossbleed duct, on the ground
and in flight up to 20 000 feet.

196
00:13:33.373 --> 00:13:37.444
Its bleed valve is operated by a
pneumatic actuator and controlled by a

197
00:13:37.484 --> 00:13:42.466
solenoid, and a check valve downstream
prevents air flowing back into the APU.

198
00:13:42.516 --> 00:13:46.476
When the crew presses APU BLEED,
the blue ON light comes on and the

199
00:13:46.516 --> 00:13:50.765
BMC checks that the APU fire pushbutton
has not been operated and that

200
00:13:50.805 --> 00:13:54.939
no leak is detected in the APU duct;
on the ground a leak is tolerated

201
00:13:54.979 --> 00:13:59.619
until the engines have started, after
which the APU has to be shut down.

202
00:13:59.669 --> 00:14:03.188
If those conditions are met,
the BMC signals the APU's

203
00:14:03.228 --> 00:14:06.870
Electronic Control Box, which
opens the bleed valve provided

204
00:14:06.910 --> 00:14:12.906
there is no APU shut-down signal and
the APU speed is stabilised at 95 %.

205
00:14:12.956 --> 00:14:16.290
The ground cart, finally,
connects through a quick-release

206
00:14:16.330 --> 00:14:19.781
access panel on the lower left
fuselage to a connector whose

207
00:14:19.821 --> 00:14:23.836
check valve has two spring-loaded
flaps that open only inwards.

208
00:14:25.486 --> 00:14:28.420
Starting the engines puts
all of this together, and it

209
00:14:28.460 --> 00:14:32.280
is the subject of the first
question of the 2020 quiz.

210
00:14:32.330 --> 00:14:34.715
With the engines stopped
there is no engine

211
00:14:34.755 --> 00:14:38.264
bleed, so the air comes from
the APU or a ground cart.

212
00:14:38.314 --> 00:14:43.692
Selecting APU BLEED opens the APU bleed
valve and, with the crossbleed selector

213
00:14:43.732 --> 00:14:49.566
in AUTO, the crossbleed valve follows it
and opens, so APU air reaches both sides.

214
00:14:49.616 --> 00:14:52.940
The air then flows in the opposite
direction to normal, from

215
00:14:52.980 --> 00:14:56.360
the manifold towards the engine,
up to the start valve, which

216
00:14:56.400 --> 00:15:00.450
opens and feeds the air starter,
which turns N2 and then N1.

217
00:15:00.500 --> 00:15:04.905
During the start sequence of an engine,
its BMC keeps that engine's pressure

218
00:15:04.945 --> 00:15:09.627
regulating valve closed electrically,
so the starting engine does not bleed.

219
00:15:09.677 --> 00:15:14.059
Once the engine is running, the start
valve closes; when the crew deselects

220
00:15:14.099 --> 00:15:18.990
APU BLEED, the engine pressure regulating
valves reopen and regulate again.

221
00:15:19.040 --> 00:15:23.596
And if a leak is detected during the
start sequence, the crossbleed valve

222
00:15:23.636 --> 00:15:28.066
stays open until the sequence ends,
so the start is not interrupted; so

223
00:15:28.106 --> 00:15:33.240
yes, both engines can be started with
APU bleed with the selector in AUTO.

224
00:15:34.890 --> 00:15:37.994
Time to practise in the
exam format: after each

225
00:15:38.034 --> 00:15:42.799
statement, pause the video, decide
true or false, and then listen.

226
00:15:42.849 --> 00:15:47.506
One: the APU can supply bleed
air in flight at any altitude.

227
00:15:47.556 --> 00:15:51.250
False: its bleed is
limited to 20 000 feet.

228
00:15:51.300 --> 00:15:56.340
Two: the APU and the ground cart feed
the right side of the crossbleed duct.

229
00:15:56.390 --> 00:15:59.909
False: both feed the left side, side 1.

230
00:15:59.959 --> 00:16:05.453
Three: selecting APU BLEED closes the
engine bleed valves automatically.

231
00:16:05.503 --> 00:16:08.662
True: that is the APU priority.

232
00:16:08.712 --> 00:16:13.892
Four: in AUTO, the crossbleed valve
opens whenever an engine bleed fails.

233
00:16:13.942 --> 00:16:17.747
False: in AUTO it only follows
the APU bleed valve, and the

234
00:16:17.787 --> 00:16:21.792
crew must select OPEN to
cross-feed from the other engine.

235
00:16:21.842 --> 00:16:27.033
Five: the hydraulic reservoirs are
pressurised from stage 9 of engine 2.

236
00:16:27.083 --> 00:16:32.449
False: from stage 8 of engine 1, with
the pneumatic manifold as a backup.

237
00:16:32.499 --> 00:16:36.970
Six: class 3 failures appear
in amber on the upper ECAM.

238
00:16:37.020 --> 00:16:43.175
False: amber is class 2, and class 3
is only for maintenance, on the MCDU.

239
00:16:43.225 --> 00:16:46.411
Seven: each BMC supervises
its own side and

240
00:16:46.451 --> 00:16:50.170
also receives some signals
from the other side.

241
00:16:50.220 --> 00:16:51.197
True.

242
00:16:51.247 --> 00:16:55.962
Eight: the engine anti-ice takes its
air from the wing anti-ice duct.

243
00:16:56.012 --> 00:16:59.188
False: each engine has its
own anti-ice valves on the

244
00:16:59.228 --> 00:17:02.794
fan case, feeding its intake
cowl with its own bleed.

245
00:17:04.444 --> 00:17:07.645
To summarise part one: air
comes from stage 5 of the

246
00:17:07.685 --> 00:17:12.006
engines, or stage 9 at idle,
from the APU up to 20 000 feet,

247
00:17:12.046 --> 00:17:16.462
or from a ground cart, and the APU
and the cart feed the left side.

248
00:17:16.512 --> 00:17:20.089
It goes to the packs, the engine
starters, wing and engine

249
00:17:20.129 --> 00:17:23.705
anti-ice, the hydraulic
reservoirs, pressurised from stage

250
00:17:23.745 --> 00:17:27.961
8 of engine 1, the water tank
and the aft cargo heating.

251
00:17:28.011 --> 00:17:31.748
On its way it passes the check
valve or the high pressure valve,

252
00:17:31.788 --> 00:17:35.348
the pressure regulating valve,
the overpressure valve and the

253
00:17:35.388 --> 00:17:39.751
precooler with its fan air valve
and temperature control thermostat.

254
00:17:39.801 --> 00:17:43.033
The APU bleed has priority
over the engines, and the

255
00:17:43.073 --> 00:17:46.757
crossbleed valve in AUTO
follows the APU bleed valve.

256
00:17:46.807 --> 00:17:50.758
Two BMCs supervise and isolate,
the ECAM shows the state,

257
00:17:50.798 --> 00:17:54.484
and the cockpit stays dark
when all is well; in part two

258
00:17:54.524 --> 00:17:58.732
we open every valve and learn
its exact logic and numbers.
